<attribute name="keyConsiderations" nodeId="a1cf9761">&lt;p&gt;
    On the earlier stage of the project benefits horizon (also called time phased benefits) is produced. It is an early
    iteration of the Benefits Realization Plan. Here is an example:
&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;
    &lt;img alt=&quot;&quot; src=&quot;./../../core.bus.common.base/workproducts/./resources/Benefits Horizon.jpg&quot; width=&quot;590&quot; height=&quot;251&quot; /&gt;
&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;
    Start by outlining benefits horizon and then fill in the details to create the plan.
&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;
    The plan is a dynamic document, which is updated throughout all stages of the program or project life cycle.
    It is informed by other benefits management work products. It should be developed in conjunction with change
    managers and program workstream leads.
&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;
    The key activities, milestones and dependencies outlined in the Benefit Realization Plan should be reviewed
    regularly along with related risks and issues as the program/project moves into the implementation phase. This
    could be done as part of the regular program/project meetings or through regular benefits realization meetings
    with workstream leads. Mitigation plans for associated risks should be kept up to date so that they can be executed if
    the risk becomes an issue. If any significant changes occur to program/project timelines or scope, the Benefits
    Realization Plan and the Business Case should be reviewed for any potential impact and updated as necessary. It’s
    critical, furthermore, to centrally coordinate and manage this risk log (the PMO or the Change Management Office can do
    this) so that all parties can have a standardized way of reporting and checking on documented risks.
&lt;/p&gt;</attribute>
